The migrated file is at `out/module.ts`. Type checking passes, 700,008 random inputs in the stated ranges match the lodash originals with zero mismatches, and the bundle drops from 12,330 to 2,173 bytes (min+gzip).

```ts
import { kebabCase, truncate } from 'es-toolkit/compat';
import { keyBy } from 'es-toolkit';

export interface Row { id: number; name: string; age: number; }
export type Config = Record<string, unknown>;

export function toSlug(label: string): string { return kebabCase(label); }
export function byNameKey(rows: Row[]): Record<string, Row> { return keyBy(rows, (row) => row.name); }
export function preview(text: string): string { return truncate(text, { length: 24 }); }
```

The own-code figure is 103 bytes, against 182 for the original.

Choices I made:

- **`toSlug` uses `kebabCase` from `es-toolkit/compat`, not the main entry.** The main-entry `kebabCase` disagrees with lodash on inputs such as `"5THwb"`: lodash gives `5-t-hwb`, es-toolkit gives `5th-wb`. The spec allows uppercase runs and digits between letters, so that gap matters. The compat version matched on every fuzzed input.
- **`preview` uses `truncate` from `es-toolkit/compat`.** The main entry has no `truncate`, and the compat one matches lodash here.
- **`byNameKey` uses `keyBy` from the main entry with an arrow function.** This is the call-shape adapter: lodash takes a property name, es-toolkit takes a function. It is three bytes of own code.
- **Nothing from lodash remains.** The file imports only from es-toolkit.

The installed packages are es-toolkit 1.53.2 and lodash 4.18.1. I checked the declarations and implementations of the functions above. I did not install anything.

The fuzz test compared the output against the lodash functions called with the same arguments as the original file, rather than against the original file itself. Its scripts are in the scratchpad directory, not in the project.

